Good afternoon Evan;

Thank you for your response, I do appreciate it.  It is responses like thes=
e that enable the Branch to make an informed, just decision regarding the s=
ervices that are being removed and the amount of the rent that should be re=
duced to balance out these services.

Before I get to the more pressing issue I wanted to let you know how the Br=
anch determines the cost of storage and laundry.  For storage; research is =
done to find something similar to the dimensions you have, and close to you=
r home.  For laundry we generally think 3 loads of laundry per week is aver=
age/fair and then calculate the dollar amount of what it would cost you to =
go to a laundromat.

Current issue.

Unfortunately I couldn=E2=80=99t access the video you showed me, I am assum=
ing its because of our security firewall protocols.  I believe our system d=
oes accept.mov files but I couldn=E2=80=99t open the ones you sent.

I did have a reply from the property manager who advised me that they have =
booked a cleaner to attend the building this week to ensure the area is cle=
an and accessible.  The Branch though cannot stop the landlord from doing t=
he renovations; only advising them that they must ensure you have access to=
 the services until May 1, 2024.   If your items are damaged because of the=
 landlord=E2=80=99s negligence (either due to the dust or security) then yo=
u have the right to file a claim through our Branch for compensation.  A he=
aring would be scheduled at which point a hearing officer would make a deci=
sion regarding your claim.

The Branch will be investigating the withdrawal of services for the other u=
nits but on an individual unit basis.

Please let me know if you are unable to access the laundry/storage due to t=
he drilling so I can follow up with the property manager.

Thank you

David Maclean
